.TH CVEC_RESERVE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_reserve \- reserve capacity for future elements (type-inferred, fatal on failure)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_reserve(v, new_capacity_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a wrapper for cvector_reserve() that calls ccol_fatal_err()
when the reservation fails, so a caller with no useful recovery for an
allocation failure does not need to test a return value. A reservation
that is too large to represent is also a failure; see NOTES for the two
limits that the macro tests it against.

The capacity grows to the next power of two at or above
new_capacity_count, with a floor of 4 elements, so the reserved capacity
is usually larger than the request. A request at or below the current
capacity does nothing, and this call never shrinks the capacity. The
macro does not change the element count: the new space is capacity and
not elements, and you can reach it only after a push into it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                   Vector to reserve capacity for
new_capacity_count  Minimum number of elements to reserve
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when the reservation
fails, which happens when the allocation fails, when the capacity is more
than ccol_max_elem_count, or when the capacity multiplied by the element
size overflows a size_t.
The macro tests both limits against the rounded-up capacity rather than
against new_capacity_count itself, so a request inside a limit can still
fail after the round-up to the next power of two. A vector of wide
elements reaches the size_t overflow limit first: that limit bounds the
capacity at SIZE_MAX divided by the element size, which for any element
wider than one byte is the lower limit of the two.
The macro evaluates new_capacity_count exactly once on every path, the
failure path included, so an expression with a side effect is safe to
pass.
The macro rounds the real capacity up to a power of two, with a minimum
of 4.
The element count stays the same; use cvec_size(3) to read it.
A reservation invalidates every pointer from an earlier
cvec_data_ptr(3) or cvec_at_ptr(3).
The macro asserts when v is NULL.
To handle a failed reservation yourself, use cvector_reserve(3) directly;
that function does not stop the program.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_reserve(vec, 100);   // capacity becomes 128, size stays 0
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    cvec_push(vec, i);   // no reallocation in the loop
}
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_reserve (3),
.BR cvec_append_array (3),
.BR cvec_push (3)
